# Credit scoring: Give Me Some Credit

Group project, Credit Scoring course.

## Objective

Estimate the risk that a borrower will experience serious financial distress (90 days or more past due) within the next two years, and group borrowers into risk categories.

## Data

The Give Me Some Credit dataset (Kaggle): 150,000 borrowers in the training set and 101,503 in the test set. The target is `SeriousDlqin2yrs`. Only 6.7% of borrowers default, so the classes are strongly imbalanced, as in a real loan portfolio.

## Method

**Variable selection**
We kept the variables whose correlation with the target was above 0.1 in absolute value. The three late-payment variables (30-59, 60-89 and 90+ days) are almost perfectly correlated with each other (0.98 to 0.99), so we kept only one of them to avoid multicollinearity. The final model uses two variables: the number of 30-59 day late payments and age.

**Risk segmentation**
After standardising the data, we applied K-Means. The elbow method gave k = 3, and the clusters were visualised with a PCA projection.

| Segment | Borrowers | Default rate |
|---|---|---|
| Low risk | 68,950 | 3.9% |
| Medium risk | 80,781 | 8.9% |
| High risk | 269 | 54.6% |

The model was then used to assign each borrower in the test set to a segment.

**Probability of default**
We trained a logistic regression on a stratified 80/20 split.

- AUC: 0.76
- 65% of defaulters are correctly identified
- One standard deviation more of 30-59 day late payments multiplies the odds of default by about 13.5, while older borrowers are less risky (odds ratio of 0.61 per standard deviation)

## Files

| File | Content |
|---|---|
| `credit_scoring_project.ipynb` | Python notebook with the full analysis |
| `GiveMeSomeCredit.zip` | Data: `cs-training.csv`, `cs-test.csv`, `sampleEntry.csv` |

Libraries: pandas, numpy, scikit-learn, matplotlib, seaborn. Unzip the archive in the same folder as the notebook before running it.
